[
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/commit/05bdd16f046e16d10f628857261c596d68dc2c51\">We have deployed a change</a> in the <code>lake exe cache</code> tool motivated by security considerations related to fetching cached artifacts produced by other users. </p>\n<p>This change, by design, reduces 'cross-pollination' of the cache, so it might result in more cache misses (and longer build times) within builds of a mathlib fork (namely: PRs). You can find more details below, including some 'technically unsafe' options that can mitigate this (at your own risk).</p>\n<p><strong>We aim for this change to be transparent for the typical usage of the cache</strong> (\"waiting for CI to finish and then calling <code>lake exe cache get</code>\"), but see the table at the end of this post for changes in your workflow that you might encounter.</p>\n<hr>\n<h4>Motivation</h4>\n<p>The Mathlib build cache holds CI-built <code>.olean</code> artifacts produced by user code in pull request CIs. </p>\n<p>This means code in a  pull request can run arbitrary code at build time (<code>#eval</code>, <code>initialize</code> blocks, and macros all elaborate during compilation), and that loading an <code>*.olean</code>file in VSCode, or running a build using a previously built <code>*.olean</code> can execute arbitrary code in your computer. </p>\n<p>Before the changes we have just deployed, a malicious fork owner could produce malicious artifacts (<code>*.olean</code>) (e.g., in a draft PR, unnoticed) and \"poison\" the cache visible from that fork, meaning that they could then present a PR (coming from the same fork) with an innocent diff that will download the malicious artifact upon <code>lake exe cache get</code>. </p>\n<p>This case is relevant to mathlib reviewers, which call <code>cache get</code> from forks when they want to build locally.</p>\n<h4>Overview of the changes</h4>\n<p>The restructuring has two sides:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Storage back-end partition.</strong> The cache infrastructure is is split, so that the <code>master</code> branch gets is dedicated 'cache universe' completely separate from the rest. This lets us protect the 'pushing' of this cache with harder boundaries.</li>\n<li><strong>Per-commit SHA scoping for fork-trust uploads.</strong> Fork-PR cache uploads are now namespaced by commit SHA. In other words, each CI run gets a unique namespace, and a malicious commit's produced artifacts cannot (by default) be re-read by a later commit on the same fork with the same input files.</li>\n</ol>\n<hr>\n<h4>Trusting a commit (SHA) to pull from</h4>\n<p>Point (2) above means that a reviewer can now actually see which code produced the <code>*.olean</code>s that are about to be fetched from the cache: the artifacts are associated with the commit SHA, so <strong>any suspicion of malicious intent can be verified by looking at the commit that produced them</strong> (the one you're standing on when calling <code>lake exe cache get</code>; but also see <code>--scope</code> below).  <strong>This is the crucial practical difference with the previous behavior</strong>.</p>\n<h4><code>master</code>-produced artifacts</h4>\n<p>Note that the changes described here only affect cached artifacts produced from a branch or a fork. The cached artifacts contributed by the master branch are always available to <code>lake exe cache get</code>.</p>\n<hr>\n<h4>Cache tool changes</h4>\n<p>The major change is that a plain <code>lake exe cache get</code> will not fetch any artifact that was previously built in the checked out fork (namely: in PRs opened from that fork that ran CI). Instead, only artifacts associated with the commit you currently have checked out will be fetched from the cache.</p>\n<p>Some extra options are included to mitigate the ‘loss of cache hits’ that results from narrowing the cache space from ‘repository’ to ‘commit’:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>lake exe cache query</code> to discover the most recent cached commit on your branch (the boolean mode <code>cache query &lt;ref&gt;</code> is also available for scripting).</li>\n<li><code>--scope=REF</code> flag on <code>cache get</code> to read from a specific commit's cache.</li>\n<li>A prominent security notice when reading at a non-default scope, so the trust transfer is visible at the point of use.</li>\n<li>The command line tool now has an <code>--unsafe</code> option that will walk history for the most recent cached fork commit and reads it as a scope automatically, instead of you copying a SHA into <code>--scope</code>. <code>--unsafe-window=N</code> will try against many such 'old' SHAs. Using this option means trusting that the history of the branch you're standing on is safe to pull.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pull request CI build time</h4>\n<p>Because every commit in a PR will build in isolation to the others (from the same PR or other PRs associated to the fork), this change can have an impact in build times, especially for PRs that change modules that are very early in the import chain. The intuition is roughly that every build will act as if it were the 'first time' the code in the PR has been built.</p>\n<hr>\n<h4>Summary: what changes for users</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Role</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">What you'll notice</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Downstream project (mathlib as a dependency)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Nothing. The cache pushed from <code>master</code> is always queried and fetched</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">PR author</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Subsequent CI builds in PRs can take longer. <code>cache get</code> after committing locally will print a heads-up note that HEAD isn't built yet; <code>cache query</code> finds a recent built commit you can scope to, or you can use <code>cache get --unsafe</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">PR reviewer (<code>gh pr checkout &lt;num&gt;</code>)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Works as before if CI has finished for the latest pushed commit. Mid-push or new local commits lose some cache, but one they can use <code>cache query</code> to partially recover.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>",
        "id": 603490680,
        "sender_full_name": "Marcelo Lynch",
        "timestamp": 1781554572
    },
